1
00:00:00,093 --> 00:00:04,215
A neural network learns by changing its weights a tiny bit at a time.

2
00:00:04,654 --> 00:00:09,095
Before each change, it needs to answer one question for every weight.

3
00:00:09,507 --> 00:00:14,148
If I nudge this weight a little, how much does the error change, and in which direction?

4
00:00:14,587 --> 00:00:17,765
The number that answers this question is called the derivative.

5
00:00:18,204 --> 00:00:19,694
It is the topic of this video.

6
00:00:20,626 --> 00:00:30,692
You will learn what a slope at a single point means, how to compute it as a limit, and the key idea behind it: close up, every smooth curve looks like a straight line.

7
00:00:31,131 --> 00:00:35,081
Then you will use a few simple rules, and find the lowest point of a curve.

8
00:00:35,506 --> 00:00:40,785
You only need school algebra: functions, graphs, and powers like x squared.

9
00:00:42,506 --> 00:00:43,702
Let's start with a straight line.

10
00:00:44,128 --> 00:00:47,984
Its slope tells you how much it rises for each step to the right.

11
00:00:48,423 --> 00:00:50,524
Slope is rise divided by run.

12
00:00:50,950 --> 00:00:55,843
This line rises two for every step of one, so its slope is two.

13
00:00:56,256 --> 00:00:58,955
A straight line has the same slope everywhere.

14
00:01:00,472 --> 00:01:04,369
Now take a curve: f of x equals x squared.

15
00:01:04,807 --> 00:01:07,188
Its steepness changes from place to place.

16
00:01:07,613 --> 00:01:09,089
Near zero it is flat.

17
00:01:09,581 --> 00:01:11,696
Far to the right it is very steep.

18
00:01:12,108 --> 00:01:15,539
So what is the slope of this curve at the single point x equals three?

19
00:01:15,964 --> 00:01:19,595
With only one point, rise over run makes no sense yet.

20
00:01:20,552 --> 00:01:21,722
Here is the trick.

21
00:01:22,161 --> 00:01:25,060
Take a second point, a small step h to the right.

22
00:01:25,499 --> 00:01:27,986
Draw the straight line through both points.

23
00:01:28,385 --> 00:01:30,632
Its slope is the average slope between them.

24
00:01:31,058 --> 00:01:33,996
With h equal to one, we go from three to four.

25
00:01:34,422 --> 00:01:37,467
The rise is sixteen minus nine, which is seven.

26
00:01:37,906 --> 00:01:38,757
The run is one.

27
00:01:39,183 --> 00:01:41,124
So the average slope is seven.

28
00:01:42,059 --> 00:01:43,535
Now shrink the step h.

29
00:01:43,841 --> 00:01:46,434
Watch the second point slide toward the first one.

30
00:01:46,886 --> 00:01:51,394
The line through both points turns, and it settles on one special line.

31
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:55,397
This line just touches the curve at x equals three.

32
00:01:55,809 --> 00:01:57,112
It is called the tangent line.

33
00:01:57,538 --> 00:01:59,825
Its slope is the slope of the curve right here.

34
00:02:01,539 --> 00:02:03,015
Let's write this as a formula.

35
00:02:03,454 --> 00:02:12,430
The average slope between x and x plus h is f of x plus h, minus f of x, all divided by h.

36
00:02:12,855 --> 00:02:14,238
The top is the rise.

37
00:02:14,651 --> 00:02:16,020
The bottom is the run.

38
00:02:16,459 --> 00:02:19,690
Now let h get closer and closer to zero.

39
00:02:20,116 --> 00:02:23,002
The value this ratio approaches is called a limit.

40
00:02:23,939 --> 00:02:30,242
The limit of this ratio, as h goes to zero, is the derivative of f at x.

41
00:02:30,668 --> 00:02:35,880
We write it as f prime of x, or as d f over d x.

42
00:02:36,306 --> 00:02:44,391
Read the formula like this: the change in the output, divided by the change in the input, for a change so small that it shrinks to nothing.

43
00:02:45,906 --> 00:02:49,203
Let's compute it for x squared at x equals three.

44
00:02:49,629 --> 00:02:52,927
With h equal to one, the ratio is seven.

45
00:02:53,352 --> 00:02:58,578
With h equal to zero point one, three point one squared is nine point six one.

46
00:02:59,004 --> 00:03:03,233
Subtract nine, divide by zero point one: six point one.

47
00:03:03,672 --> 00:03:07,847
With h equal to zero point zero one, we get six point zero one.

48
00:03:08,772 --> 00:03:11,977
Seven, six point one, six point zero one.

49
00:03:12,416 --> 00:03:14,517
The numbers are walking toward six.

50
00:03:14,956 --> 00:03:18,506
So the derivative of x squared at three is six.

51
00:03:18,919 --> 00:03:20,222
Notice the common mistake.

52
00:03:20,661 --> 00:03:23,413
We never put h equal to zero directly.

53
00:03:23,852 --> 00:03:27,456
Then we would get zero divided by zero, which means nothing.

54
00:03:27,895 --> 00:03:29,809
We only ask where the ratio is heading.

55
00:03:31,339 --> 00:03:33,719
Algebra shows why the answer is six.

56
00:03:34,145 --> 00:03:39,650
Expand x plus h, squared: x squared, plus two x h, plus h squared.

57
00:03:40,089 --> 00:03:43,294
Subtract x squared, and divide by h.

58
00:03:43,719 --> 00:03:45,860
What is left is two x plus h.

59
00:03:46,286 --> 00:03:49,610
As h goes to zero, this becomes two x.

60
00:03:50,036 --> 00:03:53,347
At x equals three, two x is six.

61
00:03:53,772 --> 00:03:56,645
The derivative of x squared is two x.

62
00:03:58,386 --> 00:04:02,774
Here is the key idea of the whole calculus part of this series.

63
00:04:03,199 --> 00:04:06,630
Zoom in on the curve near x equals three.

64
00:04:07,056 --> 00:04:07,747
Zoom in more.

65
00:04:08,173 --> 00:04:08,718
And more.

66
00:04:09,157 --> 00:04:11,537
The curve looks less and less curved.

67
00:04:11,976 --> 00:04:16,351
Close enough, it looks like a straight line, and that line is the tangent.

68
00:04:16,763 --> 00:04:20,487
Every smooth curve is almost straight when you look closely.

69
00:04:22,006 --> 00:04:25,410
So near x, we can replace the curve with its tangent line.

70
00:04:25,835 --> 00:04:33,402
In a formula: f of x plus h is approximately f of x, plus f prime of x times h.

71
00:04:33,841 --> 00:04:36,420
This is called the local linear approximation.

72
00:04:36,846 --> 00:04:40,716
The new value is the old value, plus the slope times the step.

73
00:04:41,115 --> 00:04:45,423
In this series, this is axiom C: local linearity.

74
00:04:46,372 --> 00:04:47,529
Let's check it with numbers.

75
00:04:47,955 --> 00:04:55,215
Take f of x equals x squared, x equals three, and h equal to zero point one.

76
00:04:55,667 --> 00:05:01,944
The formula says: nine, plus six times zero point one, which is nine point six.

77
00:05:02,383 --> 00:05:06,452
The true value of three point one squared is nine point six one.

78
00:05:06,891 --> 00:05:10,282
The guess is wrong by only zero point zero one.

79
00:05:11,206 --> 00:05:14,384
A second example: x cubed at x equals two.

80
00:05:14,823 --> 00:05:18,958
Its derivative there is twelve; we will see why in a moment.

81
00:05:19,397 --> 00:05:26,791
The formula guesses that two point one cubed is eight, plus twelve times zero point one, which is nine point two.

82
00:05:27,230 --> 00:05:29,716
The true value is nine point two six one.

83
00:05:30,142 --> 00:05:31,458
Again very close.

84
00:05:31,910 --> 00:05:34,557
For smaller steps, the guess gets even better.

85
00:05:35,479 --> 00:05:37,873
Notice what the step h does in the formula.

86
00:05:38,311 --> 00:05:41,530
It is multiplied by one fixed number, the slope.

87
00:05:41,942 --> 00:05:44,256
Double the step, and the change doubles.

88
00:05:44,681 --> 00:05:50,984
So, near one point, the change in the output is approximately a linear mapping of the step.

89
00:05:51,423 --> 00:05:54,734
That is the bridge between calculus and linear algebra.

90
00:05:56,459 --> 00:05:59,132
Computing limits every time would be slow.

91
00:05:59,557 --> 00:06:02,337
Luckily, a few rules do most of the work.

92
00:06:02,762 --> 00:06:04,571
The first is the power rule.

93
00:06:04,996 --> 00:06:10,847
The derivative of x to the power n is n times x to the power n minus one.

94
00:06:11,326 --> 00:06:14,744
For x squared, that gives two x, just as we found.

95
00:06:15,222 --> 00:06:17,536
For x cubed, it gives three x squared.

96
00:06:18,486 --> 00:06:21,052
The second rule is about a constant factor.

97
00:06:21,491 --> 00:06:27,156
If you multiply a function by a number c, its derivative is multiplied by c too.

98
00:06:27,595 --> 00:06:29,509
The third rule is about sums.

99
00:06:29,948 --> 00:06:33,259
The derivative of a sum is the sum of the derivatives.

100
00:06:33,698 --> 00:06:35,267
You can take each piece on its own.

101
00:06:36,772 --> 00:06:37,676
Let's use all three.

102
00:06:38,102 --> 00:06:42,184
Take f of x equals three x squared plus five x.

103
00:06:42,610 --> 00:06:47,916
The derivative of x squared is two x, so three x squared gives six x.

104
00:06:48,355 --> 00:06:50,895
The derivative of five x is five.

105
00:06:51,320 --> 00:06:54,139
So f prime of x is six x plus five.

106
00:06:54,578 --> 00:06:57,690
At x equals one, the slope is eleven.

107
00:06:59,239 --> 00:07:06,207
One more rule is so important that it gets its own video: the rule for a function inside another function.

108
00:07:06,646 --> 00:07:08,295
We will study it in video nine.

109
00:07:08,747 --> 00:07:15,196
For now, the power rule, the constant rule, and the sum rule are enough for many curves.

110
00:07:16,926 --> 00:07:18,601
Now a very useful fact.

111
00:07:19,040 --> 00:07:23,176
Here is the curve x minus two, squared, plus one.

112
00:07:23,601 --> 00:07:25,809
Watch the tangent line move along it.

113
00:07:26,208 --> 00:07:30,569
On the left, the curve goes down, and the slope is negative.

114
00:07:30,981 --> 00:07:34,492
On the right, the curve goes up, and the slope is positive.

115
00:07:34,931 --> 00:07:37,231
At the bottom, the tangent is flat.

116
00:07:38,172 --> 00:07:41,124
So at a minimum, the derivative is zero.

117
00:07:41,537 --> 00:07:42,148
Let's check.

118
00:07:42,587 --> 00:07:45,725
The derivative is two times x minus two.

119
00:07:46,164 --> 00:07:51,204
It is zero when x equals two, and that is exactly the lowest point.

120
00:07:51,656 --> 00:07:57,334
The same is true at a maximum, the top of a hill: there the tangent is also flat.

121
00:07:58,859 --> 00:08:00,761
Be careful with the opposite direction.

122
00:08:01,186 --> 00:08:05,162
A zero derivative does not always mean a minimum or a maximum.

123
00:08:05,641 --> 00:08:11,971
For x cubed, the slope at zero is zero, but the curve keeps going up on both sides.

124
00:08:12,396 --> 00:08:14,750
A zero slope only tells you where to look.

125
00:08:16,506 --> 00:08:18,474
Now let's connect this to machine learning.

126
00:08:18,886 --> 00:08:21,838
A loss is an error score: smaller is better.

127
00:08:22,250 --> 00:08:23,966
We study it in video fifteen.

128
00:08:24,378 --> 00:08:26,865
Here is a toy model with one weight w.

129
00:08:27,290 --> 00:08:30,375
Its loss is two w minus six, squared.

130
00:08:30,787 --> 00:08:37,024
Expanded, that is four w squared, minus twenty four w, plus thirty six.

131
00:08:37,972 --> 00:08:42,121
With our rules, the derivative is eight w minus twenty four.

132
00:08:42,560 --> 00:08:46,496
At w equals one, the slope is minus sixteen.

133
00:08:46,935 --> 00:08:47,666
What does that mean?

134
00:08:48,092 --> 00:08:55,366
If we nudge w up by zero point zero one, the loss changes by about minus sixteen times zero point zero one.

135
00:08:55,791 --> 00:08:58,305
That is minus zero point one six.

136
00:08:58,744 --> 00:09:02,666
The true change is minus zero point one five nine six.

137
00:09:03,959 --> 00:09:05,581
The sign tells the direction.

138
00:09:06,007 --> 00:09:10,634
A negative slope says: increase w, and the loss goes down.

139
00:09:11,073 --> 00:09:12,696
The size says how fast.

140
00:09:13,134 --> 00:09:16,698
And at w equals three, the slope is zero.

141
00:09:17,137 --> 00:09:18,773
That is the bottom, the best weight.

142
00:09:19,212 --> 00:09:21,512
Real models have millions of weights.

143
00:09:21,924 --> 00:09:26,512
In video eight we take one slope for each weight, and bundle them together.

144
00:09:28,239 --> 00:09:29,050
Now it is your turn.

145
00:09:29,475 --> 00:09:31,204
Pause the video and try this.

146
00:09:31,669 --> 00:09:35,792
First, what is the derivative of x squared at x equals five?

147
00:09:36,217 --> 00:09:42,866
Second, use the local linear approximation to guess five point one squared, without a calculator.

148
00:09:43,819 --> 00:09:44,816
Here are the answers.

149
00:09:45,255 --> 00:09:49,922
The derivative of x squared is two x, so at five it is ten.

150
00:09:50,361 --> 00:09:57,502
The guess for five point one squared is twenty five, plus ten times zero point one, which is twenty six.

151
00:09:57,941 --> 00:10:00,880
The true value is twenty six point zero one.

152
00:10:01,305 --> 00:10:02,236
Very close.

153
00:10:03,772 --> 00:10:04,530
One more question.

154
00:10:04,955 --> 00:10:08,998
Take f of x equals four x cubed, minus two x.

155
00:10:09,437 --> 00:10:13,373
What is f prime of x, and what is the slope at x equals one?

156
00:10:13,798 --> 00:10:17,309
Use the power rule, the constant rule, and the sum rule.

157
00:10:17,748 --> 00:10:18,453
Pause and try.

158
00:10:19,385 --> 00:10:23,202
The power rule turns x cubed into three x squared.

159
00:10:23,627 --> 00:10:26,008
Times four, that is twelve x squared.

160
00:10:26,433 --> 00:10:29,438
The derivative of minus two x is minus two.

161
00:10:29,851 --> 00:10:33,601
So f prime of x is twelve x squared minus two.

162
00:10:34,039 --> 00:10:38,973
At x equals one, the slope is twelve minus two, which is ten.

163
00:10:40,692 --> 00:10:42,102
Let's check all of this in Python.

164
00:10:42,540 --> 00:10:46,250
We write the average slope as a small function, called ratio.

165
00:10:46,676 --> 00:10:51,822
Then we compute it for x squared at three, with smaller and smaller steps h.

166
00:10:52,248 --> 00:10:58,285
We also test the local linear guess, the x cubed example, and the slope of our toy loss.

167
00:10:59,805 --> 00:11:03,648
This output is real; the script ran while the video was made.

168
00:11:04,087 --> 00:11:10,616
The ratios are seven, six point one, six point zero one, and six point zero zero one.

169
00:11:11,042 --> 00:11:13,263
They approach the exact value six.

170
00:11:13,702 --> 00:11:17,265
The linear guess nine point six is close to nine point six one.

171
00:11:17,704 --> 00:11:21,773
And the toy loss has slope minus sixteen at w equals one.

172
00:11:23,505 --> 00:11:24,157
In summary.

173
00:11:24,569 --> 00:11:27,561
The derivative is the slope of a curve at one point.

174
00:11:27,987 --> 00:11:36,205
It is the limit of the average slope, f of x plus h minus f of x, over h, as h goes to zero.

175
00:11:36,683 --> 00:11:39,064
For x squared, it is two x.

176
00:11:39,277 --> 00:11:47,322
Close up, every smooth curve looks straight, so f of x plus h is about f of x plus f prime of x times h.

177
00:11:48,252 --> 00:11:51,909
The power, constant, and sum rules make derivatives quick.

178
00:11:52,334 --> 00:11:56,005
At a minimum or a maximum, the derivative is zero.

179
00:11:56,417 --> 00:12:01,616
In machine learning, the derivative says how the loss changes when you nudge one weight.

180
00:12:02,042 --> 00:12:07,334
Next, in video eight: the gradient, the slope in many directions at once.

181
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:08,305
See you there.

182
00:12:10,205 --> 00:12:12,027
This video was made by David R.

183
00:12:12,466 --> 00:12:15,697
You can find more lessons at David Ramirez J dot com.

184
00:12:16,256 --> 00:12:16,974
Thanks for watching.
